WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
Single sign-on allows a relying application to depend on an identity provider for sign-in.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:14.040
In Python applications, this often means integrating with organization identity systems, customer identity platforms, or cloud identity providers.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:27.740
Federation can improve user experience and centralize identity governance, but it also creates trust boundaries around redirects, callback endpoints, claims, tokens, application assignment, and user provisioning.

4
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.700
OAuth concepts at a high level are about delegated access.

5
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.840
OpenID Connect adds an identity layer that can support sign-in and identity claims.

6
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:41.420
SAML is another federation approach used in many enterprise environments.

7
00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.080
Developers should not confuse delegated access with authentication unless an identity layer and validation model are present.

8
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:53.700
The application needs to know what it is receiving, who issued it, and what claims are trustworthy.

9
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.380
Redirects and callback endpoints deserve careful architecture and testing.

10
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:03.440
They connect the application to the identity provider and often handle security-sensitive state.

11
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.180
Mature libraries and provider guidance help reduce protocol mistakes.

12
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:17.320
Hand-rolling federation protocols is risky because small validation, state, claim, or redirect mistakes can affect the entire authentication boundary.

13
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.400
Federation governance matters after login works.

14
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:28.580
User provisioning, application assignment, group or claim mapping, tenant separation, offboarding, and role synchronization need ownership.

15
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:36.840
Claims from an identity provider may be useful identity signals, but authorization decisions should still be made deliberately in the application context.

16
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:43.900
SSO reduces password burden; it does not eliminate the need for authorization, logging, monitoring, and review.
